# LEX(1)

## NAME

lex - generator of lexical analysis programs

## SYNOPSIS

| **lex** [ **-tvfn** ] [ *file* ] ...

## DESCRIPTION

*Lex* generates programs to be used in simple lexical analysis of text. The input *files*
(standard input default) contain regular expressions to be searched for, and actions written in
C to be executed when expressions are found.

A C source program, `lex.yy.c` is generated, to be compiled thus:

> cc lex.yy.c

This program, when run, copies unrecognized portions of the input to the output, and executes
the associated C action for each regular expression that is recognized.

**Note:** v7 wrote **cc lex.yy.c -ll**, naming a library, /lib/libl.a, that held a default
*main* and *yywrap*. It is not on this system and there is nothing to link against: every
scanner supplies both itself. The rest of what that library held - *yyreject* and *yyracc*
behind REJECT, and *yyless* - is part of the generated scanner now, so nothing else is missing.

**Note:** *lex* emits declarations for the routines it calls and the ones it defines, ahead of
the code the scanner supplies, so a definition that disagrees with them will not compile. The
scanner must define

```
int yywrap(void);
```

and must not define any of *yylook*, *yyback*, *yyreject*, *yyracc*, *yyless*, *yyinput*,
*yyoutput* or *yyunput*, which the generated scanner already has. *input*, *output* and *unput*
are macros, and a scanner that wants its own may **#undef** them.

The following *lex* program converts upper case to lower, removes blanks at the end of lines,
and replaces multiple blanks by single blanks.

> ```
> %%
> [A-Z]  putchar(yytext[0]+'a'-'A');
> [ ]+$
> [ ]+    putchar(' ');
> ```

The options have the following meanings.

**-t**
: Place the result on the standard output instead of in file `lex.yy.c`.

**-v**
: Print a one-line summary of statistics of the generated analyzer.

**-n**
: Opposite of **-v**; **-n** is default.

**-f**
: `Faster` compilation: don't bother to pack the resulting tables; limited to small programs.

**Note:** v7's **-r** and **-c** selected Ratfor or C as the output language. There is no Fortran
on this machine, so the Ratfor half of *lex* is gone and both options are now unknown.

## EIGHT-BIT INPUT

A literal or a character class may contain any byte from 1 through 255, written as itself or as
an octal escape; byte 0 ends the input and cannot be matched. *Lex* masks nothing, and neither
does the scanner it generates.

**A class and `.` match one byte, not one letter.** A Cyrillic letter is two bytes in UTF-8, so
`[абв]` is a class of six bytes rather than three, and `.` matches half a letter. A literal is
the shape that behaves as expected: `"привет"` matches those twelve bytes in order.

**Note:** v7's *lex* had NCH 128 and indexed its 128-entry tables with whatever byte it read,
so a class containing a byte above 127 wrote past the end of them.

## BOUNDS

Seven tables are sized at compile time and each may be enlarged for one scanner by a line in the
definitions section. The **-v** summary prints *used*/*available* for each, named by its option,
which is how to tell which one to raise.

```
%e n     tree nodes
%p n     positions
%n n     DFA states
%a n     packed transitions
%o n     output slots
%k n     packed character classes
%t n     size of the translated character set (with a %t ... %t table)
```

## FILES

```
lex.yy.c             the scanner
/usr/lib/lex/ncform  scanner prototype for C programs
```

## ENVIRONMENT

*B6LEXFORM* names the scanner prototype, overriding /usr/lib/lex/ncform. It is how the build tree
runs a *lex* whose prototype has not been installed.

## SEE ALSO

| yacc(1)
| M. E. Lesk and E. Schmidt, *LEX - Lexical Analyzer Generator*

## DIAGNOSTICS

Bounds exceeded and syntax errors in the scanner are reported on the standard output with the
input file name and line number, and each bound names the option that raises it.

## BUGS

The output file name is fixed, so at most one *lex* process can be active in a given directory at
a time.

A rule with no start condition is active in *every* start condition, not only in INITIAL, so a
rule meant for one condition alone must say so - `<INITIAL>` included.
